From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Age of Decomp patch queue <aod-patches@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:30:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] aod: run DLAA through NVIDIA Streamline

With TOTK_TEMPORAL=dlaa (read at start, off by default; the key upstream's FSR work proposes, so one temporal backend
runs at a time) the runtime loads Streamline from the streamline folder beside the executable, after checking
NVIDIA's signature on sl.interposer.dll, sl.common.dll and sl.dlss.dll, and starts it for DLSS before the Vulkan
instance exists, with manual hooking and no over-the-air downloads. The player's nvngx_dlss.dll is never shipped: it
is found in the same folder, in an ngx folder beside the executable or where TOTK_DLSS_NGX points. The instance and
the device get the extensions DLSS lists (VK_EXT_buffer_device_address is left out: the core feature stands for it),
Streamline gets the device, and presents go through its vkQueuePresentKHR. Without any of this, DLAA logs why and
stays off.

Each frame the sub-pixel jitter turns on, the vertex constants of the first jittered draw give the camera (the
view-projection is recognised by its form, in rows 0 or 2 of c3), and before the bloom prefilter's first draw the pass
ends, the recorder thread finishes what it was given, DLSS runs in DLAA mode on the HDR colour and the depth with a
zero motion vector image (Streamline adds the camera motion from clipToPrevClip, combined in double precision), and
the result is copied back over the HDR target before the pass begins again. Camera cuts, skipped frames and size
changes reset the history; a frame without a recognised camera, or with two views, is left as it is. The Screen tab
gets an Anti-aliasing row on NVIDIA cards that have the DLLs. Streamline's headers (MIT) are in
runtime/third_party/streamline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
